        "title": "The Four-Day Workweek: From Feasibility to Implementation",
        "text": "The concept of a four-day working week has gained considerable traction in recent years, with several high-profile pilot programmes reporting remarkable results. Proponents argue that reducing working hours not only enhances employee wellbeing but also, counterintuitively, boosts productivity. A pioneering Icelandic trial conducted between 2015 and 2019 across a range of public sector workplaces found that output either remained stable or improved in the overwhelming majority of participating organisations. Workers reported lower stress levels, better work-life balance, and a marked reduction in burnout.\n\nCritics, however, contend that such findings are context-dependent and cannot be universally applied. Sectors characterised by continuous service delivery such as healthcare, emergency services, and logistics face obvious structural barriers to the adoption of a compressed week. Furthermore, economists warn that a blanket reduction in working hours, unless offset by corresponding productivity gains, risks undermining competitiveness in an already volatile global market.\n\nWhat is perhaps most striking is that the debate has shifted considerably. Rather than questioning whether a shorter week is desirable in principle, the conversation has moved to how it might be most effectively implemented across diverse industrial contexts."
